red_signatures POST + DELETE sub-resource handling' in --restore tests; only 404-suppressed cases are present; non-404 failure paths (APPLY_FAIL=1 + log emission + exit 1) are implemented at lines 670-693 but have zero test coverage despite the make_restore_fake_gh scaffolding already exposing delete_required_signatures_fail and post_required_signatures_fail fixtures. MIN-CORR-001: workflow paths includes branch-protection-log.md and STATUS.md rather than paths-ignore per dispatch-note prose; unnecessary CI runs on tooling PRs are safely short-circuited by the job body but the spec deviation is present. MIN-CORR-002: restrictions test fixture only contains URL-only fields, leaving {} after transform; test validates absence of URL fields but not presence of data fields (users/teams/apps) that real API responses include.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MAJ-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"GATE2_CAUTION_LINE emits 'wrapper SHA NOT verified against release-tag SHA' even when --expected-wrapper-sha and --i-understand-no-gate-2-verification are both supplied, in which case validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags IS called and the SHA IS verified. The persisted invocation-log block contains a false assertion; auditors reviewing the log are misled about verification posture.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"enable-required-check.sh lines 495-500: when ACK_NO_GATE2_VERIFICATION=1 AND EXPECTED_WRAPPER_SHA is non-empty, validate_expected_wrapper_sha_against_tags is called and the inline log correctly says 'the gate is re-armed against the current wrapper SHA'. But GATE2_CAUTION_LINE (lines 808-810), which is statically set whenever ACK_NO_GATE2_VERIFICATION=1, says 'wrapper SHA NOT verified against release-tag SHA'. No test covers the combined flag invocation; enable-bypass-happy-path only tests the bypass without --expected-wrapper-sha.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Audit trail in branch-protection-log.md is incorrect for the combined-flag invocation. A security auditor or future operator reading the log entry cannot determine whether the wrapper SHA was verified at Gate 3. This violates the D-047 audit-trail integrity claim.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Condition GATE2_CAUTION_LINE on both ACK_NO_GATE2_VERIFICATION=1 AND EXPECTED_WRAPPER_SHA being empty; when SHA is also supplied emit a distinct line acknowledging bypass-with-SHA-validation. Add a test case using both flags against a valid tag SHA and assert the log does not contain 'NOT verified'.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MAJ-CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE AC requires tests covering 'required_signatures POST + DELETE sub-resource handling' for --restore mode. The non-404 failure paths (APPLY_FAIL=1, log emission, exit 1) are implemented at lines 670-693 but have no test coverage, violating the AC.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"make_restore_fake_gh (lines 304-319) exposes delete_required_signatures_fail and post_required_signatures_fail fixtures but no test case writes these sentinel files. restore-delete-404 and restore-post-404 cover only the 404-suppressed paths. Lines 684-693 (DELETE non-404 failure) and 671-679 (POST non-404 failure) set APPLY_FAIL=1 and FAIL=1 then fall through to log emission; this partial-apply path is unreachable from the test suite.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If required_signatures POST or DELETE fails at runtime with a non-404 HTTP error, the script's exit-1 + log-emission guarantee is unverified. An operator relying on the audit trail after a partial-apply failure cannot trust the log entry captures the failure state correctly.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add two test cases: (1) write delete_required_signatures_fail in fake_gh_dir and assert run_restore_case exits 1 and stdout contains '- **Restore mode:** yes'; (2) write post_required_signatures_fail for a pre-state with required_signatures.enabled=true and assert the same. Both exercise the APPLY_FAIL+FAIL=1 code path and the fall-through log emission.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MIN-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Workflow on.pull_request.paths includes branch-protection-log.md and STATUS.md directly. DISPATCH-NOTE prose specifies these should appear in paths-ignore, not paths, so that tooling-slice PRs touching only these files do not trigger the workflow. The current implementation triggers a CI run that is correctly short-circuited by the job body but wastes runner minutes.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"check-invocation-log-entry.yml lines 18-20: paths lists docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md and STATUS.md. DISPATCH-NOTE states 'paths-ignore: [\\\\\\\"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024[AB]*/**\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"docs/reviews/WP-SCP-020/branch-protection-log.md\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"STATUS.md\\\\\\\"]'. A 024B-extras PR touching only STATUS.md triggers the workflow; the job body short-circuits at the tooling-slice check. The formal AC checklist only mandates paths-ignore for 024[AB]*/** (met by the 024[C-Z]*/** paths filter), so this is a spec-body vs AC-body deviation.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Minor: unnecessary CI runs on future tooling-slice PRs that touch only STATUS.md or branch-protection-log.md. Job body correctly exits 0. No enforcement bypass is possible.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"MIN-CORR-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The restore-transform test's restrictions fixture contains only URL-type fields (url, users_url, teams_url, apps_url). After the Python transform strips these, restrictions becomes {}. The test asserts URL fields are absent but does not verify that non-URL data fields (users, teams, apps arrays) which appear in real GitHub API responses are preserved. An empty {} is structurally different from a valid restrictions PUT shape.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh lines 1757-1763: pre-state.json restrictions has only url/users_url/teams_url/apps_url. Lines 1822-1829 jq assertions verify absence of URL fields but not presence of users/teams/apps. A real GitHub GET response includes all six fields; stripping URL fields from a real response leaves {users:[],teams:[],apps:[]} which is valid for the PUT API. The test fixture does not exercise this code path.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If a future adopter's restrictions object comes from a real API response with non-URL data fields, the test suite does not verify these pass through the transform correctly. This is a test coverage gap, not a runtime defect in current use (real API always returns users/teams/apps arrays).\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"nit-CORR-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"required_signatures.enabled is extracted twice in the restore path: once via Python at line 618 (RESTORE_SIGNATURES_ENABLED, for verification) and once via jq at line 666 (restore_signatures_enabled, for POST/DELETE decision). Both yield identical values from the same source JSON.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Lines 612-621: Python extracts bool((data.get('required_signatures') or {}).get('enabled', False)) -> 'true'/'false'. Line 666: jq -r '.required_signatures.enabled // false' -> 'true'/'false'. Line 742 uses the Python-extracted variable for verification; lines 667-695 use the jq-extracted one for the sub-resource call decision. Values agree for all expected GitHub API response shapes.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"No functional impact. One extraction is sufficient; the redundancy increases cognitive load when auditing the apply/verify logic.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"d7193757-f5e8-4dd0-b8b7-1bba18509ecb\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.3300203,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":16,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":120950,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":1086206,\"output_tokens\":36578,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":120950,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":33583,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":127262,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1069,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":1069},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1753,\"outputTokens\":25,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001878,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":16,\"outputTokens\":36578,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":1086206,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":120950,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.3281422999999999,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"22dc7d10-24ad-4756-9c59-1aa6b42b5d60\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
